course: dictionary
lesson: 15354
title: Soluble: dissolving and solving
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Soluble is a word with two meanings. In science, soluble means able to dissolve in a liquid, especially water. «Sugar is soluble, so it disappears when you stir it into your tea.» Sugar does not really vanish. It breaks up into tiny pieces that spread through the water.
explain: The opposite is insoluble, which means it will not dissolve. «Sand is insoluble, so it stays at the bottom of the glass.» Chalk is insoluble too. Testing which things are soluble is a fair test you can do at home with a glass of water and a spoon.
explain: The second meaning is about problems. A soluble problem is one that can be solved. «Maya said the puzzle was soluble, so she kept trying.» She believed she could work it out. Look at the clues around the word to tell which meaning the writer means.
fact: Soluble means able to dissolve in a liquid, especially water.
fact: Insoluble means not able to dissolve, so the solid stays as it is.
fact: A soluble solid, like salt, seems to disappear in water, but it is still there.
fact: You can get a dissolved solid back by evaporation: heat the water until it turns to vapour, and the solid is left behind.
fact: Soluble can also mean able to be solved, as in «a soluble problem».
exercise: «Sugar is soluble, so it disappears when you stir it into your tea.» What does soluble mean here? | Able to be seen through glass | Able to be solved | Hard and heavy | *Able to dissolve in a liquid
exercise: «Sand is insoluble, so it stays at the bottom of the glass.» What does insoluble mean? | Dissolves very quickly | Floats on the top | Turns into a gas | *Does not dissolve in the liquid
exercise: «Maya said the puzzle was soluble, so she kept trying.» What does soluble mean in this sentence? | *Able to be solved | Able to dissolve in water | Lost for ever | Too heavy to lift
test: «The soluble tablet fizzed and vanished in the glass of water.» What does soluble describe? | A tablet that is very hard to break | A tablet made of metal | A cup that holds water | *A tablet that dissolves in water
test: Sophie asks, «How do I get the salt back once it has dissolved?» What is the answer? | *Let the water evaporate, and the salt is left behind | Pour the water down the sink, and the salt goes with it | Shake the glass until the salt floats to the top | Add more sugar to bring the salt back
exam: Which of these is the best example of a soluble substance? | Pebbles | Sand | Chalk | *Sugar
